"Pending Rules and Forms Amendments (https://www.uscourts.gov/forms-rules/pending-rules-and-forms-amendments)\nciteturn1133search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; ^{1}Official Forms 101 and 106C will become effective December 1, 2026\nAlthough the rules are projected to go into effect on the dates listed, they can be delayed for various reasons or withdrawn entirely.  The status of rule and form changes being considered by advisory committees can be found in their most recent agenda books or reports.\n\n## December 1, 2026\n\n  * Appellate Form 4;\n  * Bankruptcy Rules 1007, 3018, 5009, 9006, 9014, 9017, new Rule 7043; and\n  * Evidence Rule 801.\n\nCongressional Package (PDF) - April 2026\n\nSupreme Court Package (PDF) – October 2025, as amended March 10, 2026\n\nStanding Committee Report to the Judicial Conference (PDF) – September 2025\n\nPreliminary Draft of Proposed Amendments to the Federal Rules (PDF) – August 2024\n\n## December 1, 2027\n\n  * Appellate Rule 15, 29, and 32 and the Appendix of Length Limits;\n  * Bankruptcy Rule 2002, Official Forms 101 and 106C^{1};\n  * Civil Rules 7.1, 26, 41, 45, and 81; and\n  * Criminal Rule 17.\n\nStanding Committee Report to the Judicial Conference (PDF) – September 2026 (revised August 27, 2026)^{2}\n\nPreliminary Draft of Proposed Amendments to the Federal Rules (PDF) – August 2025 \n\n^{1}Official Forms 101 and 106C will become effective December 1, 2026\n\n^{2}As set forth in the September 2026 Standing Committee Report to the Judicial Conference, the proposed amendments to Evidence Rule 609 were withdrawn prior to consideration by the Judicial Conference at its September 2026 session.\n\n## December 1, 2028\n\n  * Appellate Rule 25; \n  * Bankruptcy Rules 2003, 5005, 8011, 9006, 9036, and 9037; \n  * Civil Rules 5, 5.2, 6, and 55; \n  * Criminal Rules 11, 32, 45, 49, and 49.1; and \n  * Evidence Rules 104 and 902.\n\nPreliminary Draft of Proposed Amendments to the Federal Rules (PDF) – August 2026--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBankruptcy Rules, Forms, and Fees Changes Effective December 1, 2026 | Southern District of Georgia | United States Bankruptcy Court (https://www.gasb.uscourts.gov/news/bankruptcy-rules-forms-and-fees-changes-effective-december-1-2026)\nciteturn1133search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: yesterday; Crawled: today; # Bankruptcy Rules, Forms, and Fees Changes Effective December 1, 2026 ...   * ### Official Form 106C Schedule C: The Property You Claim as Exempt\n\n## You are here\n\n# Bankruptcy Rules, Forms, and Fees Changes Effective December 1, 2026\n\nThursday, October 1, 2026\n\n### Absent Congressional intervention, the following proposed amendments will become effective on December 1, 2025:\n\n### Amendments to the Federal Rules of Bankruptcy Procedure\n\n  * ### Bankruptcy Rule 1007\n\n  * ### Bankruptcy Rule 3018\n\n  * ### Bankruptcy Rule 5009\n\n  * ### Bankruptcy Rule 9006\n\n  * ### Bankruptcy Rule 9014\n\n  * ### Bankruptcy Rule 9017\n\n  * ### New Bankruptcy Rule 7043\n\n### These rules changes, as well as the changes to Evidence Rules 801, can be viewed on the Pending Rules and Forms Amendments page of the United States Courts website. The Congressional Package dated April 2026 contains black-line versions of the Rules and Committee Notes for a quick reference of the changes.\n\n### Changes to Official Bankruptcy Forms\n\n  * ### Official Form 101 Voluntary Petition for Individuals Filing for Bankruptcy\n\n  * ### Official Form 106C Schedule C: The Property You Claim as Exempt\n\n### Changes to the Official forms may be viewed at https://www.uscourts.gov/rules-policies/pending-rules-and-forms-amendments/pending-and-recent-changes-bankruptcy-forms.\n\n### Miscellaneous Fee Changes\n\n### Several bankruptcy miscellaneous fee changes are scheduled to take effect on December 1, 2026. CLICK HERE to view the upcoming changes. A revised bankruptcy fee schedule will be posted to this website on December 1, 2026.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUpcoming Rule, Form, and Fee Changes for December 1, 2026 | Middle District of Alabama | United States Bankruptcy Court (https://www.almb.uscourts.gov/news/upcoming-rule-form-and-fee-changes-december-1-2026)\nciteturn1133search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 4 days ago; # Upcoming Rule, Form, and Fee Changes for December 1, 2026 ... Official Form 106C is modified to provide a total of the specific dollar amount of the exemptions and adds a space on the form for the debtor's total interest in the exempt property.\n\n## You are here\n\n# Upcoming Rule, Form, and Fee Changes for December 1, 2026\n\nThursday, August 13, 2026\n\nThe following new or amended rules will become effective on December 1, 2026.\n\n1.  Rules 1007, 5009, and 9006 are amended to remove the time requirement to file a Financial Management Course certificate. The Clerk's office will send out two reminders in chapter 7 cases, at the 45-day and 90-day mark post-petition.  For chapter 13s, a second reminder will go out after the trustee files a final report an account.\n\n2.  Rule 2018 now allows the court to treat an in-court statement on the record by a creditor or representative as an acceptance of a plan.\n\n3.  Rules 9014 and 9017 are modified to allow a court to have more flexibility to hear remote testimony \"for cause and with appropriate safeguards.\"\n\n4.  New Rule 7043 allows courts to have more flexibility in hearing remote testimony in adversary proceedings for good cause in compelling circumstances and with appropriate safeguards.\"\n\nNew Forms effective on December 1, 2026.\n\n1.  Official Form 101 is amended to remind debtors and counsel to not list employer EIN numbers on the form in lieu of a Social Security Number.\n\n2. Official Form 106C is modified to provide a total of the specific dollar amount of the exemptions and adds a space on the form for the debtor's total interest in the exempt property.\n\nNew Fees effective December 1, 2026.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCongress, this change would mean that most Rule 2002 notices would no longer be required to include the last 4 digits of the debtor’s social-security or individual taxpayer-identification number. (https://www.uscourts.gov/sites/default/files/document/2026-09_bankruptcy_rules_committee_agenda_book.pdf)\nciteturn1133search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 weeks ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Approve, effective December 1, 2026, the proposed amendments to Bankruptcy Official Forms 101 and 106C, as set forth in Appendix C, for use in all bankruptcy proceedings commenced after the effective date and, insofar as just and practicable, all proceedings pending on the effective date.\nCongress, this change would mean that most Rule 2002 notices would no longer be required to include the last 4 digits of the debtor’s social-security or individual taxpayer-identification number.\n\nOfficial Form 101 (Voluntary Petition for Individuals Filing for Bankruptcy)\n\nThe proposed amendment to Official Form 101 relates to the prompt in Question 4 in Part 1 and clarifies that the question is attempting to elicit only the Employer Identification Number (EIN), if any, of the individual filing for bankruptcy and not the EIN of any other person (such as the debtor’s employer). The modification will help debtors avoid the error of providing their employer’s EIN. Because multiple debtors have the same employer, deterring such debtors from erroneously providing their employer’s EIN will avoid triggering an erroneous automated report that the debtor has engaged in repeat filings.\n\nOfficial Form 106C (Schedule C: The Property You Claim as Exempt)\n\nThe proposed amendment to Form 106C requires the debtor to add the total dollar value of the exemptions with a specific amount claimed under Column C in the form.\n\n**Recommendation:** That the Judicial Conference:\n\na. Approve the proposed amendments to Bankruptcy Rule 2002, as set forth in Appendix C, and transmit them to the Supreme Court for consideration with a recommendation that they be adopted by the Court and transmitted to Congress in accordance with the law.\n\nb. Approve, effective December 1, 2026, the proposed amendments to Bankruptcy Official Forms 101 and 106C, as set forth in Appendix C, for use in all bankruptcy proceedings commenced after the effective date and, insofar as just and practicable, all proceedings pending on the effective date.\n\n*Rules Approved for Publication and Comment*\n\nThe Advisory Committee on Bankruptcy Rules submitted proposed amendments to Bankruptcy Rules 2003, 5005, 8011, 9006(f), 9036, and 9037 with a recommendation that they\n\nRules - Page 10  \nAdvisory Committee on Bankruptcy Rules | September 23, 2026  \nPage 112 of 203--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAgenda E-19 (Summary) (https://www.uscourts.gov/sites/default/files/document/2026-09_bankruptcy_rules_committee_agenda_book_0.pdf)\nciteturn1133search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 weeks ago; Crawled: last week; Approve, effective December 1, 2026, the proposed amendments to Bankruptcy Official Forms 101 and 106C, as set forth in Appendix C, for use in all bankruptcy proceedings commenced after the effective date and, insofar as just and practicable, all proceedings pending on the effective date ............... pp.\n\n   b. Approve, effective December 1, 2026, the proposed amendments to Bankruptcy Official Forms 101 and 106C, as set forth in Appendix C, for use in all bankruptcy proceedings commenced after the effective date and, insofar as just and practicable, all proceedings pending on the effective date ............... pp. 9-10\n\n4. Approve the proposed amendments to Civil Rules 7.1, 26, 41, 45, and 81, as set forth in Appendix D, and transmit them to the Supreme Court for consideration with a recommendation that they be adopted by the Court and transmitted to Congress in accordance with the law ............................................................ pp. 13-15\n\n5. Approve the proposed amendments to Criminal Rule 17, as set forth in Appendix E, and transmit them to the Supreme Court for consideration with a recommendation that they be adopted by the Court and transmitted to Congress in accordance with the law ............................................................ pp. 18-19\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nBoxed notice: “NOTICE — NO RECOMMENDATIONS PRESENTED HEREIN REPRESENT THE POLICY OF THE JUDICIAL CONFERENCE UNLESS APPROVED BY THE CONFERENCE ITSELF.”\n</visual_element>\n\nAdvisory Committee on Bankruptcy Rules | September 23, 2026  Page 101 of 203--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRule 1007-1. Lists, Schedules, and Statements (https://www.mnb.uscourts.gov/sites/mnb/files/2026%20Proposed%20LR%20Amendments%20Package.pdf)\nciteturn1133search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last month; Amended effective January 1, 2002; January 9, 2006; May 1, 2014; December 1, 2015; July 17, 2023; December 1, 2026.] ... Subsection (b) was restyled and amended to better reflect the information that is requested on Official Form 106C.\nRule 1007-1. Lists, Schedules, and Statements\n\n(a) STATEMENT OF COMPENSATION. The statement of compensation that a debtor’s attorney must file under 11 U.S.C. § 329(a) and Federal Rule of Bankruptcy Procedure 2016(b) must conform to Local Form 1007-1(7) in chapter 7 cases and Local Form 1007-1(13) in chapter 13 cases.\n\n(b) SCHEDULE C: REAL PROPERTY. Any real property listed on Schedule C must include a legal description of the property.\n\n[Effective April 15, 1997. Amended effective January 1, 2002; January 9, 2006; May 1, 2014; December 1, 2015; July 17, 2023; December 1, 2026.]\n\n2023 Advisory Committee Notes\n\nLocal Rule 1007-1(a) was restyled to refer to 11 U.S.C. § 329(a) and Fed. R. Bankr. P. 2016(b) and eliminate language that was duplicative of Fed. R. Bankr. P. 2016(b). As amended, subsection (a) requires conformance with Local Form 1007-1 instead of substantial conformance. Subsection (b) was restyled and amended to better reflect the information that is requested on Official Form 106C. Subsection (c) was removed as duplicative of and to ensure consistency with Fed. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRecent and Proposed Amendments to Federal Rules – Annual Report 2025 (https://www.uscourts.gov/data-news/reports/annual-reports/directors-annual-report/annual-report-2025/recent-and-proposed-amendments-federal-rules-annual-report-2025)\nciteturn1133search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; At its September 2025 session, the Judicial Conference approved several amendments to the rules and one form, and one new rule, that will take effect on December 1, 2026, if approved by the Supreme Court and if Congress takes no action to the contrary. ...   * Bankruptcy Rule 2002, Official Forms 101 and 106C\n\nAt its September 2025 session, the Judicial Conference approved several amendments to the rules and one form, and one new rule, that will take effect on December 1, 2026, if approved by the Supreme Court and if Congress takes no action to the contrary. Those rules and the form are as follows:  \n\n  * Appellate Rules 29 and 32, Appendix of Length Limits, and Appellate Form 4\n  * Bankruptcy Rules 1007, 3018, 5009, 9006, 9014, 9017, and new Rule 7043\n  * Evidence Rule 801 \n\nThe amendments, upon becoming effective, primarily will do the following: \n\n  * Expand the disclosure rules related to filing an amicus brief and simplify the form for requesting in forma pauperis status in appellate cases.\n  * Change the standard for allowing remote testimony in a bankruptcy contested matter by removing the “compelling circumstances” standard borrowed from Civil Rule 43(a) (though the amended rule would still require a finding of cause and appropriate safeguards before allowing remote testimony).\n  * Provide that all prior inconsistent statements admissible for impeachment are also admissible as substantive evidence. \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNews & Announcements | Second Appellate District | District Courts of Appeal (https://appellate.courts.ca.gov/ko/node/646?page=1)\nciteturn1133search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Justice Lavin has served on the Court of Appeal since his appointment to Division Three in 2015, authoring hundreds of opin ... ... ## Court Files Miscellaneous Order 2023-02\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAnnouncements & News | U.S. Bankruptcy Court Middle District of Florida (https://www.flmb.uscourts.gov/announcements/archive.asp)\nciteturn1133search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; EFFECTIVE DECEMBER 1, 2020 - REVISION TO BANKRUPTCY COURT MISCELLANEOUS FEE SCHEDULE; AMENDMENTS TO FEDERAL RULE OF BANKRUPTCY PROCEDURE 2002(H) AND LOCAL RULE 2002-1; AND FEDERAL RULE OF CIVIL PROCEDURE 30(B)(6) (Posted: Tuesday, December 1, 2020) ...   * Official Form 106C, Schedule C: The Property You Claim as Exempt;\n"
